    </exercise>
  <!--Q7-->
  <exercise data-content-id="242f0919-e0cc-46f6-906d-e500e5353468" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="n(x)" data-solution-options="[&quot;m(x)&quot;, &quot;n(x)&quot;, &quot;neither&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Functions \(m\) and \(n\) are given by \(m(x)=(1.05)^x\) and \(n(x)=\frac{5}{8}x\). As \(x\) increases from
          0, __________ reaches 30 first.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.14.3. 
      </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q8-->
  <exercise data-content-id="a529212b-5978-4fb5-89a6-8c64dc239be0" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-retry-limit="2" data-solution="m(x)" data-solution-options="[&quot;m(x)&quot;, &quot;n(x)&quot;, &quot;neither&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Functions \(m\) and \(n\) are given by \(m(x)=(1.05)^x\) and \(n(x)=\frac{5}{8}x\). As \(x\)<emphasis>
          </emphasis>increases from 0, __________ reaches 100 first.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.14.3. 
      </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q9-->
  <exercise data-content-id="274bbeb1-10b8-4f67-8aba-fcc075b326c9" data-problem-type="multiplechoice" data-retry-limit="3" data-solution="\(g(x)\) grows faster because it is an exponential function." data-solution-options="[&quot;\\(f(x)\\) grows faster because 8 is greater than 1.2.&quot;, &quot;\\(f(x)\\) grows faster because 33 is greater than 2.&quot;, &quot;\\(g(x)\\) grows faster because it is a linear function.&quot;, &quot;\\(g(x)\\) grows faster because it is an exponential function.&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The functions \(f\) and \(g\) are defined by \(f(x)=8x+33\) and \(g(x)=2 \cdot(1.2)^x\). Which function
          eventually grows faster, \(f\) or \(g\)? Explain how you know.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 5.14.4. 
      </para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--q10-->
  <exercise data-content-id="479162ef-4347-4bc2-aa36-7089c8ada675" data-problem-comparator="text" data-problem-type="input" data-solution="2.25">
    <problem>
      <para>A line segment of length \(l\) is scaled by a factor of 1.5 to produce a segment with length \(m\). The new
          segment is then scaled by a factor of 1.5 to give a segment of length \(n\).
          
          What scale factor takes the segment of length \(l\) to the segment of length \(n\)? 
        </para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct!</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activities 5.14.1.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
